window.CANOPY_SEARCH = [{"url": "wiki/index.html", "title": "The Canopy wiki", "section": "Start here", "lede": "Guides for learning git with Canopy, recipes for everyday fixes, and a complete reference. New to git? Start with Git in ten minutes.", "headings": [{"id": "guides", "text": "Guides"}, {"id": "recipes", "text": "Recipes"}, {"id": "reference", "text": "Reference"}], "text": "New here? Read Git in ten minutes , then Your daily workflow . Already comfortable with git? Jump to the recipes, or look up a key on the Keys page. Guides  Git in ten minutes Repositories, the staging area, commits, branches and remotes, each shown where it lives in Canopy. Your daily workflow Edit, stage, commit, push, open a PR: the loop you'll do every day. Branches, merging and rebasing Why branches, how merging and rebasing differ, and what to do when they collide. Working with GitHub Connect GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI from your terminal. Recipes  Fix your last commit Typo in the message, forgot a file, or want to fold a fix into an older commit. Undo a mistake Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back \"lost\" work. Resolve a merge conflict Resolve conflicts one at a time, then finish the merge or rebase. Tidy up commits before sharing Squash, reorder, reword or drop commits with a visual interactive rebase. Find the commit that broke something Bisect to the breaking commit, then blame and file history to understand it. Work on two branches at once Check out a second branch in its own folder with worktrees. Review a pull request Check out a PR, read the diff and checks, then approve or request changes. Reference  Configuration Every setting in config.toml, with its default. Command line Flags, arguments and environment variables. Themes The five built-in themes and their colors. Troubleshooting Fixes for the problems people actually hit. FAQ Short answers to common questions."}, {"url": "wiki/git-basics.html", "title": "Git in ten minutes", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The handful of ideas that make git make sense, and where to see each one in Canopy.", "headings": [{"id": "repo", "text": "A repository is a project with a memory"}, {"id": "three-places", "text": "Your changes live in three places"}, {"id": "commits", "text": "A commit is a saved snapshot"}, {"id": "branches", "text": "A branch is a line of work"}, {"id": "remotes", "text": "A remote is the same repo somewhere else"}, {"id": "safety", "text": "You can almost always undo"}], "text": "A repository is a project with a memory  A repository (repo) is a folder whose history git tracks. Everything git knows lives in a hidden .git folder inside it. Run canopy in any repo and the Home tab ( 1 ) shows where things stand. canopy — Home Your changes live in three places  The working tree is the files you edit. Changes here are listed under Changes on the Changes tab ( 2 ). The staging area (also called the index) is where you gather what goes into the next commit. Staged files appear under Staged . History is the commits themselves, on the History tab ( 3 ). Staging is what lets you commit part of your work. In Canopy you can stage a whole file with space , or step into its diff with ↵ and stage single lines. canopy — Changes A commit is a saved snapshot  A commit records the staged changes with a message saying why. Each commit points to the one before it, which is how history forms a chain. Press c , write a short summary (Canopy counts to 50 characters for you), and press ↵ . Good messages say why : \"Fix crash when the repo has no commits\" beats \"update main.rs\". A branch is a line of work  A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, your current branch moves forward. Branches let you try something without disturbing main . The Branches tab ( 4 ) lists them; n makes a new one and space switches to it. HEAD means \"where you are now\"; History shows it as HEAD → main . A remote is the same repo somewhere else  A remote (usually called origin ) is a copy of the repo on a server such as GitHub. Push ( P ) sends your commits there; pull ( p ) brings others' commits to you; fetch ( f ) checks what's new without changing your files. The header shows ↑2 ↓1 when you're 2 commits ahead and 1 behind. You can almost always undo  Git rarely forgets anything. z undoes your last commit, reset, merge or checkout, and the Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere you've been so you can go back further. See Undo a mistake . Learn as you go: keep teach mode on (it is by default). The footer shows the git command behind every action you take, so the ideas on this page turn into commands you'll recognize."}, {"url": "wiki/daily-workflow.html", "title": "Your daily workflow", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The loop you'll repeat every day: pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a pull request.", "headings": [{"id": "tips", "text": "Tips"}], "text": "Start up to date. Open Canopy in your repo and press p to pull. Home tells you if you're behind. Make a branch for your change. Go to Branches ( 4 ), press n , and name it after the change, like fix-login-timeout . Canopy switches to it. Edit your files in your editor as usual. Canopy notices changes within a few seconds; ⌃R refreshes right away. Review and stage. On Changes ( 2 ), read each file's diff on the right. Press space to stage a file, or ↵ to go into the diff and stage only some lines. Commit. Press c , write a summary, press ↵ . Repeat steps 3–5 as you go: small commits are easier to review and to undo. Push. Press P . The first push of a new branch also sets it up to track the remote. Open a pull request. Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press n . Give it a title and a description of what changed and why. Watch CI. Actions ( 0 ) shows your branch's runs updating live. Home's GitHub card also shows whether checks pass. Tips  Not ready to commit but need to switch tasks? S stashes your changes; pop them back from the Stash tab ( 5 ). Committed too early? On Changes, A amends the last commit. See Fix your last commit . Forgot where you were? Home's Next steps says what to do next."}, {"url": "wiki/branches.html", "title": "Branches, merging and rebasing", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Why branches matter, the two ways to combine them, and what to do when they collide.", "headings": [{"id": "why", "text": "Why branch"}, {"id": "merge", "text": "Merging"}, {"id": "rebase", "text": "Rebasing"}, {"id": "conflicts", "text": "When they collide"}, {"id": "tracking", "text": "Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\""}], "text": "Why branch  A branch keeps a change separate until it's ready. main stays working while you experiment, and you can drop a branch without affecting anything else. Branches are cheap: make one for every change. Merging  Merging brings another branch's commits into yours. If both branches moved on, git makes a merge commit with two parents; History draws it as ○ where two lines join. On Branches ( 4 ), select the branch to bring in and press M . Rebasing  Rebasing replays your commits on top of another branch, as if you'd started from its latest commit. History stays a straight line, with no merge commit. Select the branch to rebase onto and press r . Which should I use? Merge when a branch's history matters as its own story, or when others already have your commits. Rebase to keep your own unshared work tidy before opening a PR. Never rebase commits other people have pulled: rebasing rewrites them. When they collide  If both branches changed the same lines, git stops and asks you to choose. The header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE ), and Home says what to do. Follow Resolve a merge conflict . Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\"  A branch's upstream is the remote branch it syncs with, like origin/main . The Branches tab shows ↑1 ↓2 (1 commit to push, 2 to pull) or ✓ when in sync. u sets the upstream if it's missing; pushing a new branch with P sets it for you."}, {"url": "wiki/github-flow.html", "title": "Working with GitHub", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Connect Canopy to GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI without leaving the terminal.", "headings": [{"id": "connect", "text": "Connect GitHub"}, {"id": "prs", "text": "Pull requests (8)"}, {"id": "issues", "text": "Issues (9)"}, {"id": "actions", "text": "Actions (0)"}], "text": "Connect GitHub  Canopy uses the official GitHub CLI, gh , with your own login. Install and log in once: $ brew install gh $ gh auth login Canopy never installs anything for you. If gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs say exactly what to run. Pull requests (8)  See every open PR with its checks and review state. f cycles open, yours, review requested and all. The side panel shows the description, checks, reviews and comments; D adds the diff. canopy — Pull requests Issues (9)  Browse issues with labels and comments. n files a new one, C comments, X closes (or reopens). Actions (0)  Runs for your branch, updating live. Select a failed run to see the failing step and the end of its log, then R to re-run it. For the full key list, see the GitHub page ."}, {"url": "wiki/fix-last-commit.html", "title": "Fix your last commit", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "A typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix that belongs in an older commit.", "headings": [{"id": "message", "text": "Change the last commit's message"}, {"id": "forgot", "text": "Add a forgotten file"}, {"id": "older", "text": "Fold a fix into an older commit"}], "text": "Change the last commit's message  On Changes ( 2 ), press A (amend). The dialog opens with the current message. Edit it and press ↵ . Add a forgotten file  On Changes ( 2 ), stage the file with space . Press A , keep the message, press ↵ . The file is now part of the last commit. Fold a fix into an older commit  Stage the fix. On History ( 3 ), select the commit it belongs to and press F . Canopy makes a fixup! commit. To squash it in, press i on the commit before it and start the rebase; see Tidy up commits . Already pushed? Amending rewrites the commit, so the next push needs force. Canopy offers \"force push (with lease)\", which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime. Only do this on branches nobody else is using."}, {"url": "wiki/undo.html", "title": "Undo a mistake", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back work you thought was lost.", "headings": [{"id": "z", "text": "The quick way: z"}, {"id": "further", "text": "Going further back: the reflog"}, {"id": "discard", "text": "Throw away uncommitted edits"}], "text": "The quick way: z  z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, and tells you exactly what it will do before it does it: After a commit: the commit is undone and its changes come back staged , ready to fix and commit again. After a checkout: you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase: the branch moves back and your uncommitted changes are kept. Going further back: the reflog  The Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits no branch points to any more. To get back to any of them: Open Reflog ( 7 ) and find the entry from before the mistake. The side panel shows that commit. Press r and choose Mixed to move your branch there and keep your files as they are, or Hard to make your files match it too. Throw away uncommitted edits  On Changes, select the file and press d . Canopy tells you exactly what will be lost and asks first. This one can't be undone, because git never saved those edits."}, {"url": "wiki/conflicts.html", "title": "Resolve a merge conflict", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "When a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stops because both sides changed the same lines.", "headings": [], "text": "You'll know you have a conflict because the header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE or CHERRY-PICK ) and Home says how many files are conflicted. Go to Changes ( 2 ). Conflicted files are listed first, under Conflicts . Select one and press ↵ . The panel shows each conflict with ours (your side) and theirs (the side you're bringing in). For each conflict, press o to keep yours, t to take theirs, or b to keep both. j / k move between conflicts. Canopy writes the file as you go. When a file has no conflicts left, Canopy stages it for you. When all files are resolved, press C to continue the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts Need something in between? Press e to open the file in your editor, fix it by hand, save, and come back. Changed your mind about a choice? u puts the conflict markers back. Want out entirely? X aborts and returns everything to how it was."}, {"url": "wiki/clean-history.html", "title": "Tidy up commits before sharing", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Squash, reorder and drop commits with a visual interactive rebase.", "headings": [], "text": "Before opening a PR, you might want to turn \"wip\", \"fix typo\" and \"actually fix it\" into one clear commit. That's an interactive rebase . On History ( 3 ), select the oldest commit you want to change and press i . The rebase editor lists those commits, oldest at the top. For each one choose: p pick : keep it as is. s squash : combine with the commit above, keeping both messages. f fixup : combine with the commit above, dropping this message. e edit : stop at this commit so you can change it. d drop : remove it. Move commits up and down with K and J . Press ↵ to run it. If it stops on a conflict, resolve it as in Resolve a merge conflict and press C . Don't rewrite shared history. Only tidy commits you haven't pushed, or ones on a branch that's only yours. If the rebase goes wrong, z undoes it."}, {"url": "wiki/find-a-bug.html", "title": "Find the commit that broke something", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Bisect to the commit that introduced a bug, then use blame and file history to understand it.", "headings": [{"id": "bisect", "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit"}, {"id": "blame", "text": "Blame: who changed this line, and why"}, {"id": "history", "text": "File history"}], "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit  Commit or stash your changes (bisect checks out other commits). On History ( 3 ), select a commit where things still worked and press b . Your current commit counts as broken. Git checks out a commit halfway between. Test it: build it, run it, whatever shows the bug. Press b and answer good or bad (or skip if you can't test it). The header shows about how many steps are left. When git finds the culprit, choose Finish and show it . Canopy stops bisecting and selects that commit in History. Blame: who changed this line, and why  On Changes, or on a file inside a commit's diff, press B . Each line shows the commit that last changed it. ↵ jumps to that commit; B again shows the file as it was just before that change, so you can keep digging. canopy — Blame File history  Press L on a file to see only the commits that touched it, following renames. esc goes back to the full history."}, {"url": "wiki/two-branches.html", "title": "Work on two branches at once", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Keep a second branch checked out in its own folder with worktrees.", "headings": [], "text": "Switching branches means stashing or committing half-done work. A worktree avoids that: a second folder for the same repository, with a different branch checked out. Go to Branches ( 4 ) and press ] until you reach Worktrees . Press n and type a branch name (existing or new). Canopy creates the folder next to your repo: acme-app + fix/login becomes acme-app-fix-login . Press ↵ to open it in Canopy, or open the folder in your editor. When you're done, select it and press d to remove it. If you deleted the folder by hand, x tidies up."}, {"url": "wiki/review-a-pr.html", "title": "Review a pull request", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Check out a PR, read it, run it, and leave a review.", "headings": [], "text": "Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press f until it shows review requested . Select the PR. The side panel has the description, checks and existing reviews. Press D to read the diff. Want to run it? Press space to check out its branch. Press r and choose Approve , Comment , or Request changes . Write your review (Markdown works) and press ⌃S . If it's approved and checks pass, M merges it: merge commit, squash, or rebase. canopy — Pull requests"}, {"url": "wiki/config.html", "title": "Configuration", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Every setting in config.toml, with its default.", "headings": [{"id": "keys", "text": "Remapping keys"}, {"id": "custom", "text": "Custom commands"}], "text": "The config file is ~/.config/canopy/config.toml (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopy/config.toml ). canopy --config-path prints where Canopy looks; canopy --example-config prints a starter file. Every setting is optional. theme \"canopy\" Color theme: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord or light . See Themes . teach_mode true Show the git command behind each action in the footer. T toggles it while running. confirm_destructive true Ask before discarding changes, hard resets, force deletes and similar. compact false Tighter layout: less padding and no gaps between panels. splash true Play the tree animation when Canopy starts (any key skips it). mac_key_symbols macOS: true , else false Show modifier keys as ⌃ ⌥ ⇧ instead of ctrl- / alt- . nerd_font false Use Nerd Font icons (only turn on if your terminal font has them). workspace_dirs [] Folders the Workspace tab scans for repositories, e.g. [\"~/code\"] . Empty means the folder above the current repo. workspace_depth 3 How many folders deep to look for repositories. log_page_size 300 Commits History loads at a time (more load as you scroll). gh_program unset Path to the GitHub CLI if it isn't gh on your PATH . [keys] empty Remap actions. See below. [[custom_commands]] none Run your own commands from a key. See below. Remapping keys  Map an action's name to a key or a list of keys. Names are in the last column of the Keys page. A key you assign is taken away from whatever used it before, and Canopy warns you if that leaves an action without a key. [keys] toggle_stage = \"s\" commit = [\"c\", \"ctrl-s\"] push = \"alt-p\" Key names: a character ( x , X , ? ), space , enter , esc , tab , backtab , arrows ( up …), pageup , home , f1 – f12 , with ctrl- and/or alt- in front. Custom commands  [[custom_commands]] key = \"X\" cmd = \"git push --force-with-lease\" description = \"force push (safely)\" confirm = true The command runs with sh -c in the repository's root. Its output is shown when it finishes. description is what the command palette and help show; confirm asks first."}, {"url": "wiki/cli.html", "title": "Command line", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Flags, arguments and environment variables.", "headings": [{"id": "env", "text": "Environment variables"}], "text": "$ canopy [OPTIONS] [PATH] PATH Repository to open. Defaults to the current folder. -w , --workspace DIR Start in the Workspace tab, scanning DIR for repositories. -t , --theme THEME Use a theme for this run: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light . --config-path Print where Canopy reads its config, then exit. --example-config Print a starter config, then exit. -V , --version Print the version. -h , --help Print help. Environment variables  CANOPY_CONFIG Read the config from this file instead of the default location. XDG_CONFIG_HOME If set, the config lives at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopy/config.toml . CANOPY_GH Path to the GitHub CLI (same as gh_program in the config). VISUAL , EDITOR The editor e opens files in (falls back to vi )."}, {"url": "wiki/themes.html", "title": "Themes", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Five built-in themes. Set one with theme = \"…\" in the config, pass -t, or cycle live with ⌃T.", "headings": [], "text": "Themes use 24-bit color. If colors look wrong, your terminal may not support it; see Troubleshooting ."}, {"url": "wiki/troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Fixes for the problems people actually run into.", "headings": [{"id": "not-a-repo", "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\""}, {"id": "gh", "text": "GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in"}, {"id": "option", "text": "Option shortcuts don't work"}, {"id": "glyphs", "text": "Borders or symbols look misaligned"}, {"id": "colors", "text": "Colors look wrong or washed out"}, {"id": "push", "text": "Push was rejected"}, {"id": "config", "text": "\"Config error, using defaults\""}, {"id": "bug", "text": "Something else"}], "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\"  You passed a folder that isn't a repo. Run canopy inside a repository, or use canopy -w ~/code to pick one from the Workspace tab. GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in  Install the GitHub CLI and log in, then reopen Canopy: brew install gh , gh auth login . If gh lives somewhere unusual, set gh_program in the config. Option shortcuts don't work  Canopy understands both terminals that send Option as Alt/Meta and macOS Terminal's default, where Option types a special character on a US keyboard layout. With other keyboard layouts, turn on Use Option as Meta key (Terminal → Settings → Profiles → Keyboard) or the equivalent in iTerm2 (Profiles → Keys → Left Option key: Esc+). Borders or symbols look misaligned  Canopy only draws characters that are one column wide in standard monospace fonts. If lines still don't meet, your terminal font may be missing box-drawing characters: try SF Mono, Menlo, JetBrains Mono or Fira Code. In iTerm2, turn off Treat ambiguous-width characters as double width . Colors look wrong or washed out  Canopy uses 24-bit (\"true\") color. Some terminals, including older versions of macOS Terminal, don't support it and approximate the colors; iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and Alacritty do support it. Trying another theme with ⌃T can help too. Push was rejected  Someone pushed to the branch before you. Press p to pull first; if your branch has diverged, P offers to pull with rebase, pull with merge, or force push with lease. \"Config error, using defaults\"  The config file has a typo. The message names the line. canopy --example-config prints a valid starting point. Something else  Open an issue with what you did, what you expected, and your terminal and OS."}, {"url": "wiki/faq.html", "title": "FAQ", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Short answers to common questions.", "headings": [{"id": "does-canopy-replace-git", "text": "Does Canopy replace git?"}, {"id": "is-it-safe-to-use-on-important-repositories", "text": "Is it safe to use on important repositories?"}, {"id": "does-it-send-my-code-anywhere", "text": "Does it send my code anywhere?"}, {"id": "does-it-work-on-linux-windows", "text": "Does it work on Linux? Windows?"}, {"id": "can-i-use-it-with-gitlab-or-bitbucket", "text": "Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?"}, {"id": "why-and-but-no-shortcuts", "text": "Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?"}, {"id": "how-do-i-update", "text": "How do I update?"}], "text": "Does Canopy replace git?  No. Canopy runs the real git for everything, so your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all work, and you can mix it freely with the command line. Is it safe to use on important repositories?  Canopy asks before anything destructive, and z undoes most actions. Everything it does is a normal git command; turn on teach mode to see each one. Does it send my code anywhere?  No. Git runs locally. GitHub features use your own gh login and only contact GitHub when you open a GitHub tab or act on one. Does it work on Linux? Windows?  macOS and Linux, yes. Windows isn't supported yet. Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?  All the git features work with any remote. The GitHub tabs are GitHub-only for now. Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?  Terminals keep the Command key for themselves (copy, paste, new tab) and don't pass it to programs, so Canopy uses Control and Option. How do I update?  brew upgrade canopy , or re-run the cargo install command with --force ."}, {"url": "index.html", "title": "Canopy", "section": "Home", "lede": "Canopy is a beautiful, powerful git and GitHub dashboard for your terminal. Friendly for your first commit, fast for your thousandth.", "headings": [], "text": "Everything git does One screen for the whole repository. What changed, what's staged, where your branch stands, and what to do next. Every action is one key away, and every key is discoverable. Stage single lines Open any diff, then stage or unstage a line, a range, or a hunk. Craft commits that say exactly one thing. Undo anything Press z to undo the last commit, reset, merge, or checkout. An undone commit comes back as staged changes. Conflicts, one at a time See ours and theirs for each conflict. Keep one, the other, or both. The file is staged once nothing's left. History that makes sense A commit graph with search, file history that follows renames, blame, and a visual interactive rebase. Bisect, guided Mark a commit that worked, answer good or bad at each step, and Canopy points at the commit that broke things. Every repo, one view The Workspace tab shows every repository in your code folders: branch, changes, and sync state. GitHub built in Pull requests, reviews, issues, and CI runs, through the official GitHub CLI and your own login. Yours to configure Five themes, remappable keys, and custom commands on any key. The palette finds every action by name. For everyone New to git? Fluent in git? Both welcome. Canopy explains itself while you use it, and gets out of the way once you don't need it to. If you're learning Next steps on Home tell you, in plain words, what state you're in and which key moves you forward. Teach mode shows the real git command behind every action. Confirmations say exactly what a destructive action will throw away. A safety net: undo with z , and a reflog view of everywhere you've been. If you live in the terminal Line-level staging, interactive rebase, fixups, cherry-picks, and bisect. Vim-style keys you can remap, plus custom commands on any key. Real git underneath: your hooks, config, signing, and credential helpers all just work. A single ~4&nbsp;MB binary that starts instantly and never blocks while git runs. # you press space on a line in a diff, and the footer shows: $ git apply --cached --unidiff-zero --whitespace=nowarn - # stdin: a patch with just the selected lines # you press z after a commit: $ git reset --soft 1f3a9c2 ← what that just ran Roadmap Where Canopy is growing. Built in the open, one phase at a time. Read the changelog → v0.1 The git dashboard v0.2 Remappable keys, tags &amp; remotes, conflicts one by one v0.3 GitHub PRs, issues &amp; CI · blame, bisect, worktrees v0.4 The Canopy wiki: guides, recipes, reference Later A desktop GUI Install Canopy Star on GitHub"}, {"url": "features.html", "title": "Features", "section": "Product", "lede": "A tour of every tab in Canopy, with real screenshots.", "headings": [{"id": "home", "text": "1 · Home"}, {"id": "changes", "text": "2 · Changes"}, {"id": "history", "text": "3 · History"}, {"id": "conflicts", "text": "Conflicts"}, {"id": "refs", "text": "4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees &amp; submodules"}, {"id": "stash", "text": "5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog"}, {"id": "workspace", "text": "6 · Workspace"}, {"id": "undo", "text": "Undo"}, {"id": "bisect", "text": "Bisect"}, {"id": "palette", "text": "Command palette &amp; teach mode"}], "text": "On this page Home Changes History Conflicts Branches &amp; more Stash &amp; Reflog Workspace Undo Bisect Palette &amp; teach mode 1 · Home Your repository at a glance: the branch and whether it's in sync, what's staged and changed, recent commits with a graph, commit activity, and your branches. Next steps reads the state of the repo and tells you, in plain words, what to do next and which key does it. canopy — Home 2 · Changes Everything you've changed, grouped as conflicts, changes, and staged. space stages or unstages a file, a does all of them, d discards (after telling you exactly what you'll lose). Press ↵ to step into the diff. There, space stages just the line under the cursor , v selects a range, and ↵ takes the whole hunk. s switches to a side-by-side view. canopy — Changes When you're ready, c opens the commit dialog, with a live length guide for the summary line. A amends the last commit instead. 3 · History The commit graph for your branch, with branch and tag labels. / filters by message, author or hash. On any commit: check it out, cherry-pick C , revert t , reset r (soft, mixed or hard), tag T , branch from it n , or fold staged changes into it F . canopy — History i opens a visual interactive rebase from the selected commit: reorder with J / K , and pick, squash, fixup, edit or drop each commit. L on a file shows its history (following renames) and B shows blame, where B again steps back to the version before that line changed. canopy — Blame Conflicts When a merge or rebase stops on conflicts, the header says so and Home tells you what to do. Open a conflicted file with ↵ and resolve it one conflict at a time: o keeps yours, t takes theirs, b keeps both. The file is written as you go and staged when nothing's left; u puts the markers back if you change your mind. Then C continues the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts 4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees &amp; submodules One tab, five views: [ and ] switch between them. Branches: local and remote, with ahead/behind. Checkout, create, rename, delete, merge M , rebase r , set upstream. Tags: create ( name: message makes an annotated tag), push, delete here or everywhere. Remotes: add, rename, change URL, fetch one, remove. Worktrees: check out another branch in its own folder, open it in Canopy, remove or prune. Submodules: see which are in sync, update one or all, open one. canopy — Branches 5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog Stash shelves your uncommitted work ( S from anywhere) so you can switch tasks; apply, pop or drop it later. Reflog lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits you \"lost\" to a reset. Reset back to any entry from there. 6 · Workspace Point Canopy at your code folders ( workspace_dirs in the config, or canopy -w ~/code ) and see every repository at once: branch, uncommitted changes, conflicts, and whether it's ahead or behind. ↵ opens one; F fetches them all. canopy — Workspace Undo z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, using git's reflog. Canopy tells you what it's about to do first: After a commit : the commit is undone and its changes come back staged . After a checkout : you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase : the branch moves back, and your uncommitted changes are kept. Bisect Something broke, but you don't know when. In History, select a commit where it still worked and press b . Git checks out a commit halfway between; test it, press b , and answer good or bad. The header counts down the steps left, and when git finds the culprit, Canopy selects it in History. Command palette &amp; teach mode : or ⌃P opens the palette: type a few letters of any action and press ↵ . ! runs any git command you like. With teach mode on ( T toggles it), the footer shows the exact git command behind each action you take. canopy — Command palette Next GitHub"}, {"url": "github.html", "title": "GitHub", "section": "Product", "lede": "Pull requests, issues and CI runs in your terminal, through the GitHub CLI.", "headings": [{"id": "setup", "text": "Setup"}, {"id": "prs", "text": "8 · Pull requests"}, {"id": "issues", "text": "9 · Issues"}, {"id": "actions", "text": "0 · Actions"}, {"id": "home", "text": "On Home"}, {"id": "privacy", "text": "What Canopy sends"}], "text": "On this page Setup Pull requests Issues Actions On Home What Canopy sends Setup GitHub features need gh , logged in. Canopy never installs anything itself; if gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs show exactly what to run. $ brew install gh $ gh auth login That's it. Open Canopy in any repository whose remote is on github.com. 8 · Pull requests Open pull requests with their checks ( ✓ 2/2 , ✗ 1 failed ), review state, author, and age. f cycles between open, yours, review requested, and all. The side panel shows the description, every check, reviews, and comments; D adds the diff. canopy — Pull requests space Check out the pull request's branch n Open a pull request for the current branch (push it first with P ) r Review: approve, comment, or request changes C Comment M Merge: merge commit, squash, or rebase (warns when checks fail) X Close o Open in your browser 9 · Issues Open, assigned-to-you, or all issues, with labels and comment counts. Read the full discussion in the side panel, then n to file one, C to comment, X to close (or reopen a closed one). canopy — Issues 0 · Actions Workflow runs for your branch ( f for all branches), refreshing every few seconds while any run is in progress. Select a failed run to see which job and step failed, and the end of its log, cleaned up so you can read it. R re-runs the failed jobs. canopy — Actions On Home Home's GitHub card shows your branch's pull request, whether its checks pass, and how many reviews are waiting for you. Next steps picks it up too: failing checks, requested changes, or a green, approved PR ready to merge. What Canopy sends Nothing of its own. Every GitHub action is a gh command run on your machine with your login. Teach mode shows each one, for example gh pr merge 12 --squash --delete-branch . GitHub is only contacted when you open a GitHub tab, refresh, or act, never in the background while you work on other tabs. Previous Features Next Get started"}, {"url": "changelog.html", "title": "Changelog", "section": "Product", "lede": "What's new in each Canopy release.", "headings": [{"id": "next", "text": "What's next"}], "text": "On this page v0.3.0 v0.2.0 v0.1.0 What's next v0.3.0 Latest September 2026 GitHub Pull requests tab ( 8 ): checks, reviews, diff; check out, create, review, comment, merge, close. Issues tab ( 9 ): browse, file, comment, close and reopen. Actions tab ( 0 ): live run status, failing steps and log tail, re-run failed jobs. Home shows your branch's PR, its checks, and reviews waiting for you. Git File history ( L ) that follows renames, and blame ( B ). Guided bisect ( b ). Worktrees and submodules views in the Branches tab. Polish Ctrl and Option shortcuts: ⌃Q / ⌥Q quit from anywhere, ⌥1 … ⌥0 jump to tabs, ⌥← / → cycle them, plus Option versions of commit, pull, push, fetch, stash and undo. Borders line up in every terminal: Canopy only draws glyphs that are one column wide everywhere. The tab bar shortens itself on narrow terminals. v0.2.0 September 2026 History loads more commits as you scroll. Remap any key with [keys] in the config. Tags and remotes views in the Branches tab. Resolve merge conflicts one at a time: ours, theirs, or both. v0.1.0 September 2026 The first release: Home, Changes with line-level staging, History with a commit graph and interactive rebase, Branches, Stash, Workspace, Reflog. Undo with z , teach mode, command palette, five themes, custom commands. What's next v0.4: the Canopy wiki , with guides, recipes, and a full reference. Later: syntax-highlighted diffs, and a desktop GUI. Previous Keys"}, {"url": "get-started.html", "title": "Get started", "section": "Docs", "lede": "Install Canopy with Homebrew or Cargo, run it from source, and configure it.", "headings": [{"id": "brew", "text": "Install with Homebrew"}, {"id": "cargo", "text": "Install with Cargo"}, {"id": "source", "text": "Run from a clone of the source"}, {"id": "first", "text": "First run"}, {"id": "config", "text": "Configuration"}, {"id": "themes", "text": "Themes"}, {"id": "update", "text": "Updating"}], "text": "On this page Homebrew With Cargo From source First run Configuration Themes Updating Install with Homebrew The easiest way on a Mac: $ brew install shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy Copy Then run canopy inside any git repository. Install with Cargo If you have Rust installed ( rustup.rs ), Cargo can build and install Canopy straight from GitHub. It lands in ~/.cargo/bin , which rustup already put on your PATH : $ cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy canopy-git-tui Copy Why canopy-git-tui ? That's the package name (the name canopy was already taken on crates.io). The command it installs is still canopy . Run from a clone of the source To try the latest code, or hack on it: $ git clone https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy.git $ cd canopy # run it without installing (the -- passes arguments to canopy) $ cargo run --release -- ~/code/your-repo # or install your local build as `canopy` $ cargo install --locked --path crates/canopy cargo run builds and starts Canopy in one step; the first build takes a minute or two, later ones are quick. Without a path after -- , Canopy opens the repository you're in. Since you're in the Canopy clone itself, that's a handy way to try it on a real history. Running the tests: cargo test --workspace . First run The first time, Canopy shows a short welcome with the keys that matter and writes a starter config. From then on: 1 – 0 switch tabs; the footer always lists the keys that work right now. ? shows every key for the current screen; : searches every action by name. q quits. ⌃Q , ⌥Q and ⌃C quit from anywhere, even with a dialog open. Configuration The config file lives at ~/.config/canopy/config.toml ( canopy --config-path prints it; canopy --example-config prints a template). theme = \"canopy\" # canopy | catppuccin | gruvbox | nord | light teach_mode = true # show the git command behind each action confirm_destructive = true compact = false # true: tighter layout, no gaps between panels splash = true # the tree animation when Canopy starts workspace_dirs = [\"~/code\"] # folders the Workspace tab scans [keys] # remap any action (names are on the Keys page) toggle_stage = \"s\" commit = [\"c\", \"ctrl-s\"] [[custom_commands]] key = \"X\" cmd = \"git push --force-with-lease\" description = \"force push (safely)\" confirm = true Themes Five built in: canopy (forest greens), catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , and light . Set one in the config, pass -t nord , or cycle live with ⌃T . Updating $ brew upgrade canopy # Homebrew $ cargo install --locked --force --git https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy canopy-git-tui # Cargo Previous GitHub Next Keys"}, {"url": "keys.html", "title": "Keys", "section": "Docs", "lede": "Every Canopy key, generated from the app itself.", "headings": [{"id": "k-global", "text": "Everywhere"}, {"id": "k-status", "text": "Changes"}, {"id": "k-diff", "text": "Diff panel (after ↵)"}, {"id": "k-conflict", "text": "Conflict panel"}, {"id": "k-log", "text": "History"}, {"id": "k-branches", "text": "Branches"}, {"id": "k-tags", "text": "Tags"}, {"id": "k-remotes", "text": "Remotes"}, {"id": "k-worktrees", "text": "Worktrees"}, {"id": "k-submodules", "text": "Submodules"}, {"id": "k-stash", "text": "Stash"}, {"id": "k-workspace", "text": "Workspace"}, {"id": "k-reflog", "text": "Reflog"}, {"id": "k-pulls", "text": "Pull requests"}, {"id": "k-issues", "text": "Issues"}, {"id": "k-runs", "text": "Actions"}], "text": "On this page Everywhere Changes Diff panel Conflict panel History Branches Tags Remotes Worktrees Submodules Stash Workspace Reflog Pull requests Issues Actions Keys are shown the Mac way: ⌃ is Control, ⌥ is Option (Alt on other keyboards), ⇧ is Shift. Option shortcuts work in any terminal, including macOS Terminal's default setup where Option types special characters. ⌃Q and ⌥Q quit from anywhere, even inside a dialog. In Canopy itself, Linux shows ctrl-q / alt-q instead; set mac_key_symbols in the config to choose. Everywhere ? ⌥H f1 help help : ⌃P ⌃K command palette palette q ⌃Q ⌥Q ⌃C quit quit 1 ⌥1 go to home goto_home 2 ⌥2 go to changes goto_changes 3 ⌥3 go to history goto_history 4 ⌥4 go to branches goto_branches 5 ⌥5 go to stash goto_stash 6 ⌥6 go to workspace goto_workspace 7 ⌥7 go to reflog goto_reflog 8 ⌥8 go to pull requests goto_pull requests 9 ⌥9 go to issues goto_issues 0 ⌥0 go to actions goto_actions tab ⌥→ ⌃→ next tab next_screen ⇧tab ⌥← ⌃← previous tab prev_screen ⌃R ⌥R refresh refresh ⌃L redraw the screen redraw c ⌥C ⌃S commit commit f ⌥F fetch fetch p ⌥P pull pull P ⌥⇧P push push z ⌥Z undo last action undo S ⌥S stash changes stash_push b bisect: find the commit that broke something bisect ! ⌥G run git command raw_git T toggle teach mode toggle_teach ⌃T ⌥T cycle theme cycle_theme k ↑ up up j ↓ down down ⌃U pageup page up page_up ⌃D pagedown page down page_down g home top top G end bottom bottom esc back back Changes space stage/unstage toggle_stage a stage/unstage all stage_all d discard changes discard ↵ l open enter A amend last commit amend e open in editor open_editor i add to .gitignore ignore o resolve: keep ours take_ours t resolve: take theirs take_theirs C continue merge/rebase continue_op X abort merge/rebase abort_op L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame Diff panel (after ↵) space stage/unstage line(s) stage_line ↵ stage/unstage hunk stage_hunk v select range range_select n ] next hunk next_hunk N [ previous hunk prev_hunk s toggle split view toggle_side_by_side h ← back back L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame Conflict panel o keep ours for this conflict keep_ours t take theirs for this conflict keep_theirs b keep both (ours, then theirs) keep_both j ↓ n next conflict next_conflict k ↑ N previous conflict prev_conflict u restore conflict markers restore_conflict e open in editor open_editor h ← back back History ↵ l open enter space checkout commit checkout_commit C cherry-pick cherry_pick t revert commit revert r reset to commit reset_menu i interactive rebase from here rebase_interactive n new branch here branch_from_commit T tag commit tag_commit F fixup staged into commit fixup_commit y copy hash copy_hash / search search Branches space ↵ checkout checkout n new branch new_branch d delete branch delete_branch M merge into current merge r rebase current onto rebase R rename branch rename_branch u set upstream set_upstream / search search ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Tags space ↵ checkout tag checkout_tag n new tag at HEAD new_tag d delete tag delete_tag P push tag push_tag ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view / search search Remotes f fetch this remote fetch_remote n add remote add_remote d remove remote remove_remote e change remote URL edit_remote_url R rename remote rename_remote ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Worktrees ↵ space open worktree in Canopy open_worktree n new worktree for a branch new_worktree d remove worktree remove_worktree x prune missing worktrees prune_worktrees ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Submodules ↵ space open submodule in Canopy open_submodule u update submodule to recorded commit update_submodule U update"}];
